\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
Ridge regression (Two-norm regularization)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Problem:
 
\series default
Add regularisation term 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda||\mathbf{w}||^{2}_{2}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda>0$
\end_inset

 and the problem of ridge regression becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\mathbf{w}}} & =\text{arg}\min_{\mathbf{\mathbf{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}}\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{i=1}(y_{i}-\mathbf{w}'\boldsymbol{\tilde{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{i}))^{2}+\lambda||\mathbf{w}||^{2}_{2}\\
 & =\text{arg}\min_{\mathbf{\mathbf{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}}\frac{1}{N}\left(\mathbf{y}-X\mathbf{w}\right)'\left(\mathbf{y}-X\mathbf{w}\right)+\lambda\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{w},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Solution:
 
\series default
The first order condition (of the lagrangian) are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\mathbf{w}}|_{\mathbf{w}=\hat{\mathbf{w}}} & =\frac{2}{N}\left(X'(\mathbf{y}-X\hat{\mathbf{w}})+\lambda\hat{\mathbf{w}}\right)=0\\
\hat{\mathbf{w}} & =(X'X+\lambda I_{d+1})X'\mathbf{y}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Note that for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 the solution approaches that of OLS and for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 the solution goes to 0 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{w}}\rightarrow\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Intuitively this is because weight-elements other than 0 are penalised a lot.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Choosing regularization paramter:
 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 can be chosen by cross validation (choosing the 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{*}$
\end_inset

 minimzing CV risk).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
Equivalence theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Ridge regression can be seen as solving
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{arg }\min_{\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}}\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{i=1}(y_{i}-\boldsymbol{\tilde{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{i}))^{2}\;\text{s.t. }||\mathbf{w}||_{2}\leq C,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
becuase this is equivalent to the ridge regression problem:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{arg}\min_{\mathbf{\mathbf{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}}\frac{1}{N}\left(\mathbf{y}-X\mathbf{w}\right)'\left(\mathbf{y}-X\mathbf{w}\right)+\lambda\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{w},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=f(C)$
\end_inset

 depending on C.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
This intepretation is useful because of what the restricted hypothesis space (where 
\begin_inset Formula $||\mathbf{w}||_{2}\leq C$
\end_inset

) tries to implement for the affine linear hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\tilde{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{i})$
\end_inset

).
 Formally let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\phi}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 (where 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the dimensions of the raw underlying data which the featurespace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 is created from).
 be continuous and Lipschits with constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 meaning:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
||\boldsymbol{\tilde{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{1})-\boldsymbol{\tilde{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{2})||_{2}\leq\alpha||\mathbf{x}_{1}-\mathbf{x}_{2}||_{2},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}$
\end_inset

.
 Then the functions 
\begin_inset Formula $h:\mathbb{R}^{d_{0}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{1}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{H}_{C}=\left\{ h(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{w}'\boldsymbol{\tilde{\phi}}(\mathbf{x})\mid\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}\text{ and }||\mathbf{w}||_{2}\leq C\right\} ,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
will be Lipschitz with constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C\cdot$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 In other words 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 limits how fast the prediction rule / hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 can change.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
Lasso regression (One-norm regularization)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Much the same as ridge regression but where we add the l1-norm regularization (to reduce the number of active dimensions - i.e.
 increase number of weights equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}$
\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{\boldsymbol{\mathbf{w}}} & =\text{arg}\min_{\mathbf{\mathbf{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}}\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{i=1}(y_{i}-\mathbf{w}'\boldsymbol{\tilde{\phi}}(\mathbf{x}_{i}))^{2}+\lambda||\mathbf{w}||_{1}\\
 & =\text{arg}\min_{\mathbf{\mathbf{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}}\frac{1}{N}\left(\mathbf{y}-X\mathbf{w}\right)'\left(\mathbf{y}-X\mathbf{w}\right)+\lambda\sum^{d}_{j=1}|w_{j}|
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Comment on double descent 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Sometimes when letting the number of parameters grow 
\begin_inset Formula $d>>N$
\end_inset

 - while including a regularization term like 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda||\mathbf{w}||^{2}_{2},\;\lambda>0$
\end_inset

 then at some point testing error can start diminishing rather than keep increasing.
 This can even result in lower empirical test loss than models with 
\begin_inset Formula $d<N$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The name comes from the stylized fact that test loss decreases when adding model dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 is low.
 At some point the model overfits and testing empirical risk increases.
 And then the magic can enter for 
\begin_inset Formula $d>>N$
\end_inset

 where the test loss again decreases!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Linear Classification 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The task of linear classification is to predict the correct class 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}\in\{\mathcal{C}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{C}_{m}\},\:2\leq m$
\end_inset

 from the input 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

.
 One approach is to learn the discriminant functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{k}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1,\dots,m$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}_{i}=h(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\text{arg}\max_{k\in\{1,\dots,m\}}\delta_{k}(\mathbf{x}_{i})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We specify affine linear discriminant functions
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\delta(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\mathbf{w}'\boldsymbol{\phi}(\mathbf{\tilde{x}}_{i}),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}=\boldsymbol{\phi}(\mathbf{\tilde{x}}_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the features always augmentet to hold a 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 (so that the model is affine linear - just like in linear regression) and can also hold many other features created from the raw data 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\mathbf{x}_{i}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Logistic regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Binary case
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}\in\{1,-1\}$
\end_inset

 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow[0,1]$
\end_inset

 be the sigmoid function defined by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta(s)=\frac{1}{1+\exp\{s\}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Deriving the binary sigmoid:
 
\series default
The sigmoid encodes 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}$
\end_inset

 as the log ratio of probabilities.
 i.e.
 on log scale how relatively probable 
\begin_inset Formula $Y=1$
\end_inset

 is compared to 
\begin_inset Formula $Y=-1$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x} & =\ln\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=\mathbf{x}\right)}{\mathbb{P}\left(Y=-1\mid X=\mathbf{x}\right)}\\
\exp\left\{ \mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\}  & =\frac{\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=\mathbf{x}\right)}{1-\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=\mathbf{x}\right)}\\
\left(1-\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=\mathbf{x}\right)\right)\exp\left\{ \mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\}  & =\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=\mathbf{x}\right)\\
\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=\mathbf{x}\right) & =\frac{\exp\left\{ \mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }{1+\exp\left\{ \mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }\\
 & =\frac{1}{1+\exp\left\{ -\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }\equiv\theta(\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Properties of sigmoid in the binary case:

\series default
 In the binary case we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta(y\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x})=\begin{cases}
\theta(\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}) & \text{if }y=1\\
1-\theta(\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}) & \text{if }y=-1
\end{cases}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is easy to show:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
1-\theta(\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}) & =\frac{1+\exp\left\{ -\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }{1+\exp\left\{ -\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }-\frac{1}{1+\exp\left\{ -\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }\\
 & =\frac{\exp\left\{ -\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }{1+\exp\left\{ -\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }\\
 & =\frac{1}{1+\exp\left\{ \mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}\right\} }\\
 & =\theta(-\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x})\\
 & =\theta(y\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}),\;y=-1
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Log likelihood optimization:
 
\series default
Therefore the negative log likelihood function becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
-\ln L(\mathbf{w}) & =-\ln\prod^{N}_{n=1}\mathbb{P}\left(Y=y_{n}\mid X=\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w}\right)\\
 & =-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ln\mathbb{P}\left(Y=y_{n}\mid X=\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w}\right)\\
 & =-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ln\frac{1}{1+\exp\{-y_{n}\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}\\
 & =\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ln[1+\exp\{-y_{n}\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}],
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which when divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the objective function of the unconstrained minimisation problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{MLE}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}}\underbrace{-\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ln[1+\exp\{-y_{n}\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}]}_{Q(\mathbf{w})},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with first order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}Q(\mathbf{w}) & =\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\frac{1}{1+\exp\{-y_{n}\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}\left(-\exp\{-y_{n}\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}\right)\left(-y_{n}\mathbf{x}_{n}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\frac{y_{n}\mathbf{x}_{n}}{1+\exp\{y_{n}\mathbf{w}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}=0,
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which does not have any closed form solution.
 However,
 because the problem is convex this can be solves globally using 
\emph on
gradent descent
\emph default
.
 The only caveat is the case where data is perfectly linearly seperable.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(\mathbf{w})\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 and the problem has no solution.
 Instead one can solve the L2-norm regularized problem (just like in linear regression) on the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{MLE,L2}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}}Q(\mathbf{w})+\lambda||\mathbf{w}||^{2}_{2},\;\lambda>0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which is a stricly convex problem\SpecialChar endofsentence
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Multiple classification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Defining the probability distribution:
 
\series default
The only difference is how the probability distribution is defined.
 The MLE machinery + gradient descent always stays the same.
 For each category
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{Y}\in\{\mathcal{C}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{C}_{m}\},\:2\leq m
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
we define
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(Y=y\mid X=\mathbf{x};\mathbf{w}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{w}_{m}\right)\equiv\frac{\exp\{\delta_{k}(\mathbf{x})\}}{\sum^{m}_{j=1}\exp\{\delta_{j}(\mathbf{x})\}}=\frac{\exp\{\mathbf{w}_{k}'\mathbf{x}\}}{\sum^{n}_{j=1}\exp\{\mathbf{w}_{j}'\mathbf{x}\}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Log likelihood:
 This 
\series default
yields the nogative log likelihood function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
-\ln L(\mathbf{w}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{w}_{m}) & =-\ln\prod^{N}_{n=1}\mathbb{P}\left(Y=y_{n}\mid X=\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{w}_{m}\right)\\
 & =-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ln\mathbb{P}\left(Y=y_{n}\mid X=\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{w}_{m}\right)\\
 & =-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ln\frac{\exp\{\mathbf{w}_{k}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}{\sum^{n}_{j=1}\exp\{\mathbf{w}_{j}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Identification:
 
\series default
Since adding any vector 
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 
\series default
to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}_{k}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\dots,m$
\end_inset

 does not change the (negative log) likelihood:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\exp\{(\mathbf{w}_{k}+\mathbf{a})'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}{\sum^{n}_{j=1}\exp\{(\mathbf{w}_{j}+\mathbf{a})'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}=\frac{\exp\{\mathbf{a}\}\exp\{\mathbf{w}_{k}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}{\exp\{\mathbf{a}\}\sum^{n}_{j=1}\exp\{\mathbf{w}_{j}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}=\frac{\exp\{\mathbf{w}_{k}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}}{\sum^{n}_{j=1}\exp\{\mathbf{w}_{j}'\mathbf{x}_{n}\}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
then we either need to add a regularization term or normalise one of the class-weights.
 For the latter solution a common choice is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}_{m}=\mathbf{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

.
 The remaining model-weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{1},\dots,\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{m-1}$
\end_inset

 are solved for using gradient descent.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Optimization:
 Gradient descent methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Gradient descent:
 
\series default
In short gradient descent iteratively updates the weights using a first order taylor expansion around the current point 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}^{(t)}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $(t)$
\end_inset

 indicates the iteration number.
 The goal is to minimize 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(\mathbf{w})$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{w}^{(t+1)} & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}+\mathbf{\Delta_{w}}^{(t)}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}Q|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with learning rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta>0$
\end_inset

 determining how large steps are in proportion to the gradient.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Often we add a 
\emph on
momentum term
\emph default
 (in an attempt to improve performance) which intuitively works like a heavy ball.
 When a heavy ball has gained momentum from accelerating down a steep hill it will roll over local minimums and further down towards the global minimum (hopefully).
 Formally we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{w}^{(t+1)} & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}}+\mu\mathbf{\Delta_{w}}^{(t-1)}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla_{\mathbf{w}}Q|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}}+\mu\left(\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\mathbf{w}^{(t-1)}\right),
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with momentum parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu>0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Stochastic gradient descent:
 
\series default
Esentially the same algorithm but where the gradient is calculated using one 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbf{x}_{n},y_{n})\in S$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is the training data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Minibatch gradient descent:
 
\series default
The same but where the gradient is calculated on a random subset 
\begin_inset Formula $S'\subset S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\lang american
Random Forest
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Binary decision trees (definitions)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
CART trees 
\series default
are introduced and used in MLA and are popular binary classification / regression trees.
 Each 
\emph on
inner node
\emph default
 in a CART tree evaluates one dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d\in\{1,\dots,D\}$
\end_inset

 of the dataset using a threshold value 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 and splits the data into
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L_{d,\theta}=\left\{ (\mathbf{x},y)\in S\mid x_{d}<\theta\right\} \text{ and }R_{d,\theta}=\left\{ (\mathbf{x},y)\in S\mid x_{d}\geq\theta\right\} ,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{d}$
\end_inset

 is element 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}$
\end_inset

.
 The subset 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{d,\theta}$
\end_inset

 is then sent to the left daughter node and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{d,\theta}$
\end_inset

 to the right.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
The splits are determined by maximising the information gain
\series default
 or equivalently minimising the impurity measure 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 at the dataset at that note 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 Formally we would like to choose the dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 as well as the threshold value 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 solving
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\text{arg }\max_{d,\theta}Q(S)-\frac{|L_{d,\theta}|}{|S|}Q(L_{d,\theta})-\frac{|R_{d,\theta}|}{|S|}Q(R_{d,\theta})\\
=\text{arg }\min_{d,\theta}G_{d,\theta}(S)=|L_{d,\theta}|Q(L_{d,\theta})+|R_{d,\theta}|Q(R_{d,\theta}).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
This is however impractical (and maybe even impossible) to solve on a computer and we therefore use some heuristic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Leaf nodes
\series default
 are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=1,\dots,|T|$
\end_inset

 and they define 
\emph on
regions
\emph default
.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 reaches leaf node 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{R}_{\tau}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
Regression trees
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Trees can not only classify observations 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 into categories but also estimate its value (if the label is continuous)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}_{i}=T(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\sum^{|T|}_{\tau=1}c_{\tau}\mathbb{I}(\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathcal{R}_{\tau})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
With squared loss 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{y}-y)^{2}$
\end_inset

 the constants minimising empirical risk are the respective empirical means of each leafnode 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{\tau}=\frac{1}{N_{\tau}}\sum_{\mathbf{x}_{n}\in\mathcal{R}_{\tau}}y_{n},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
with 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbf{x}_{n},y)\in S$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\tau}=|\{(\mathbf{x}_{n},y)\in S\mid\mathbf{x}_{n}\in\mathcal{R}_{\tau}\}|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
Classification trees
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Neural Networks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Neuron
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Pre-Activation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The preactivation of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
a_{i}(\mathbf{x}) & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}_{i}'\mathbf{x}_{+1},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 denotes the argmentet vector 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{x}_{+1}=\begin{bmatrix}1 & x_{1} & \cdots & x_{d}\end{bmatrix}'.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Activation and output 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying a (non-linear) activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 yields the output of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{i}(\mathbf{x})=h_{i}(a_{i}(\mathbf{x}))=h_{i}\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is each neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has its own weights (and bias) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 and activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The latter is usually the same for all neurons except the output-neurons (in the top layer).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Activation functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Linear activation function:
 
\series default
For the network structure to have any reason to exist the activation functions 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

 must be non-linear.
 If not the neural network 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{K}$
\end_inset

 will simply be a linear function of the inputs aswell.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To see this note that each neuron in the inputlayer would output the following functional form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
h\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\right) & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\underbrace{aw_{ij}}_{\tilde{w}_{ij}}x_{j}+\underbrace{aw_{i0}+b}_{\tilde{w}_{i0}}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{ij}x_{j}+\tilde{w}_{i0},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and since each pre-activation is simply a affine linear function of the previous affine linear functions all later neurons will output something of the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
h\left(a\left(\begin{bmatrix}z_{1} & z_{2} & \cdots & z_{K}\end{bmatrix}\right)\right) & =h\left(\sum^{K}_{k=1}w_{k}z_{k}+w_{0}\right)\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}z_{k}+\hat{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\tilde{w}_{k0}\right)+\hat{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\underbrace{\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\tilde{w}_{k0}+\hat{w}_{0}}_{\bar{w}_{0}}\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\sum^{d}_{j=1}\hat{w}_{k}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\bar{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\bar{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}x_{j}\underbrace{\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\hat{w}_{k}\tilde{w}_{kj}\right)}_{\bar{w}_{j}}+\bar{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\bar{w}_{j}x_{j}+\bar{w}_{0}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then also goes for all other layers recieving these outputs (by the exact same argument as above).
 So in summary:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

 is linear for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 then there is no purpose to the neural network structure and the simple affine linear model of linear regression fits the model just as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Well known activation functions (greatest hits).
 
\series default
Here i list some of the most common activation functions.
 Some are more common for outputlayers for example logistic activation and are rarely used in hidden layers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Step / Threshold
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\mathbb{I}(a>0),
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Hyperbolic tangent
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=tanh(a),
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}\in(-1,1)$
\end_inset

 and with a visual form much like 
\emph on
alternative sigmoid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Rectified linear unit (ReLU)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\max(0,a),
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

.
 Note that there the derivative of the loss function wrt.
 pre-activation weights is uninformative in the regrion 
\begin_inset Formula $a<0$
\end_inset

.
 By that i mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial h_{i}/\partial w_{ij}=0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{w})<0$
\end_inset

 and so it does not help us adjust weights to decrease loss in these negative regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Fermi / logistic
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\frac{1}{1+\exp\left\{ -a\right\} },
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Alternative sigmoid
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\frac{a}{1+|a|},
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Exponential LU (ELU,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\begin{cases}
a & a\geq0\\
\alpha(\exp\{a\}-1) & a<0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}\in(-\alpha,\infty)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Gradient descent
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To optimize a neural network with error function 
\begin_inset Formula $E=\sum^{N}_{n=1}E_{n}$
\end_inset

 (as with loglikelihood functions) we introduce gradient descent.
 The bare form of this algorithm is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{w}^{(t+1)} & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}+\mathbf{\Delta_{w}}^{(t)}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with learning rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta>0$
\end_inset

 determining how large steps are in proportion to the gradient.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Often we add a 
\emph on
momentum term
\emph default
 (in an attempt to improve performance) which intuitively works like a heavy ball.
 When a heavy ball has gained momentum from accelerating down a steep hill it will roll over local minimums and further down towards the global minimum (hopefully).
 Formally we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{w}^{(t+1)} & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}}+\mu\mathbf{\Delta_{w}}^{(t-1)}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}}+\mu\left(\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\mathbf{w}^{(t-1)}\right),
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with momentum parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu>0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The question now really is how to calculate the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}}$
\end_inset

 given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial E}{\partial w_{ij}}=\sum^{N}_{n=1}\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial w_{ij}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

 contains all the edges connection the output of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to the input of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 which is scaled by 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 This is answered in the next section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Backpropagation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal is to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial w_{ij}}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Why not for 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\neq\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

?
 These weights simply do not exist.
 The model only has weights symbolizing the strength of the connect from neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 There is no connection from 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 back to 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 so this 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{ji}$
\end_inset

 does not exist.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Applying the chain rule to every element of the error function 
\begin_inset Formula $E=\sum^{N}_{n=1}E_{n}$
\end_inset

 yields the following.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{n}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the network.
 and that any 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{j}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore the chain rule yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial w_{ij}} & =\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}\frac{\partial z_{i}}{\partial a_{i}}\frac{\partial a_{i}}{\partial w_{ij}}\\
 & =\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}h_{i}'(a_{i})\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{j}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the last step uses that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{i}(\mathbf{z})\equiv h_{i}(a_{i}(\mathbf{z}))\implies\frac{\partial z_{i}}{\partial a_{i}}=h'_{i}(a_{i}),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}$
\end_inset

 contains all the inputs from the previous layer to neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 For the last factor we used that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a_{i}(\mathbf{z}_{n})=w_{i0}+\sum^{M}_{m=1}w_{im}\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{m}\implies\frac{\partial a_{i}}{\partial w_{ij}}=\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{j}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore the missing factor to be calculated is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}$
\end_inset

 which we will now turn to.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The ouput layer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the output layer 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}$
\end_inset

 can be directly calculated.
 To see this let the loss function be the sum of squared errors 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
E=\frac{1}{2}\sum^{N}_{n=1}||f(\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w})-\mathbf{y}_{n}||^{2} & =\frac{1}{2}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\left(\sqrt{\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\left[f(\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w})\right]_{k}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{k}\right)^{2}}\right)^{2}\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\left[f(\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w})\right]_{k}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{k}\right)^{2}\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{k}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{k}\right)^{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in V_{output}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore we have (for 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in V_{output}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}} & =\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}\frac{1}{2}\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{k}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{k}\right)^{2}\\
 & =\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{i}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{i}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}(a_{i})=a_{i}\implies h_{i}'(a_{i})=1$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in V_{output}$
\end_inset

 we thus have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial E}{\partial w_{ij}} & =\sum^{N}_{n=1}\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}h_{i}'(a_{i})z_{j}\\
 & =\sum^{N}_{n=1}\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{j}\\
 & =\sum^{N}_{n=1}\left(\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{i}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{i}\right)\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{j}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Hidden layers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For hidden layers we apply the apply the chainrule to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{j}} & =\sum_{k\text{ in }(j,k)\in\mathcal{A}}\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{k}}\frac{\partial z_{k}}{\partial z_{j}}\\
 & =\sum_{k\text{ in }(j,k)\in\mathcal{A}}\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{k}}\frac{\partial z_{k}}{\partial a_{k}}\frac{\partial a_{k}}{\partial z_{j}}\\
 & =\sum_{k\text{ in }(j,k)\in\mathcal{A}}\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{k}}h_{k}'(a_{k})w_{kj}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The partial derivatives of the layer just before 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{ouput}$
\end_inset

 is then calculated by pluging in 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{k}}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in V_{output}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,k)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Then the third layers uses the second layers calculated 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{j}}$
\end_inset

 and so on.
 This is sometimes referred to as the backwards pass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Principal Component Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Definitions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Dimensions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is the dimensionality of the original dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 (sampled from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

).
 As an example consider the face database where each picture is 
\begin_inset Formula $92×122$
\end_inset

 pixels.
 Then the dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 of the input space is 
\begin_inset Formula $D=10304$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d<D$
\end_inset

 is the dimensionality of the outputspace of PCA i.e.
 the dimensionality we have reduced the dataset to using PCA.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the number of observations in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 can then be defined by stacking all 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 observations 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}'\in\mathbb{R}^{D}$
\end_inset

 as the rows to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times D}$
\end_inset

.
 This is very much like the design matrix in linear regression).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $U_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times d}$
\end_inset

 takes in 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 dimensional enocded data and outputs 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 dimensional reconstructed data (where we add 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}$
\end_inset

 for the final output 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{dec}(\mathbf{z}_{i})=\mathbf{b}+U_{d}\mathbf{z}_{i}$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The objective of PCA
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal of PCA is to take a dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 sampled from
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 with datapoints 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{D},$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\dots,$
\end_inset

 and linearly map them into 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $d<D$
\end_inset

 while minimizing the reconstruction error
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
J=\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}||\mathbf{x}_{n}-f_{dec}(\mathbf{z}_{n})||^{2}_{2},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{dec}(\mathbf{z}_{n})=\mathbf{b}+U_{m}\mathbf{z}_{n},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{b}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times d}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\emph on
decoding
\emph default
 function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words we are looking for the 
\emph on
encoding 
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{enc}:\mathbb{R}^{D}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $d<D$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\min_{\mathbf{b},U_{d}\{\mathbf{z}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{z}_{N}\}}\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}||\mathbf{x}_{n}-f_{dec}(\mathbf{z}_{n})||^{2}_{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Optimal reconstruction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 (with the columns 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{1},\dots,\mathbf{u}_{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}$
\end_inset

) be an orthonomal basis (spanning the the whole 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{D}$
\end_inset

 with unit lenght basisvectors)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{u}_{k}'\mathbf{u}_{j}=\begin{cases}
1 & k=j,\\
0 & k\neq j.
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

 Then every vector / observation 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}$
\end_inset

 can be represented as a linear combination of the basisvectors in 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{x}_{i}=\sum^{D}_{j=1}a_{j}\mathbf{u}_{j}.\label{eq:newbasis1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now want to determine 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Premultiply 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:newbasis1}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 by any of the new basisvectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{k}',$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\{1,\dots,D\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{u}_{k}'\mathbf{x}_{i} & =\mathbf{u}_{k}'\sum^{n}_{j=1}a_{j}\mathbf{u}_{j}=\sum^{n}_{j=1}\mathbf{u}_{k}'a_{j}\mathbf{u}_{j}=\sum^{n}_{j=1}a_{j}\mathbf{u}_{k}'\mathbf{u}_{j}\\
 & =\underbrace{a_{1}\mathbf{u}_{k}'\mathbf{u}_{1}+\cdots+}_{=0}a_{k}\mathbf{u}_{k}'\mathbf{u}_{k}\underbrace{+\cdots+a_{\ell}\mathbf{u}_{k}'\mathbf{u}_{\ell}}_{=0}\\
 & =a_{k},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where we have used the pairwise orthogonality of the basis vectors and the unit length of any 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{k}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{k}'\mathbf{u}_{k}=||\mathbf{u}_{k}||^{2}_{2}=1^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Inserting 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{k}=\mathbf{u}_{k}'\mathbf{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\{1,\dots,D\}$
\end_inset

 into 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:newbasis1}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{x}_{i}=\sum^{d}_{j=1}\left(\mathbf{u}_{j}'\mathbf{x}_{i}\right)\mathbf{u}_{j}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that this only works because of the orthonormal restriction on the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_body
